% Background theory: the ISO 3166 uniqueness rule.
%
% ISO 3166 assigns one code to an area, so two areas listed by the
% extension are two places.  The extension asserts none of this: the
% rule is the parties', and a verdict resting on an instance of it is
% withdrawable by abandoning the rule.
%
% Distinctness is between areas rather than codes.  Where the
% extension records two codes for one area, a country code and a
% subdivision code, the area appears once below under its country
% code; a rule applied to the codes would contradict the identity
% the extension publishes.
%
% The rule is stated here and instantiated by the problems.  A
% problem naming two of the areas below carries the inequation
% between them as a bt_ assertion, so that a refutation cites the
% instance it used.
%
% Areas: 249
%
% AD, AE, AF, AG, AI, AL, AM, AO, AQ, AR, AS, AT
% AU, AW, AX, AZ, BA, BB, BD, BE, BF, BG, BH, BI
% BJ, BL, BM, BN, BO, BQ, BR, BS, BT, BV, BW, BY
% BZ, CA, CC, CD, CF, CG, CH, CI, CK, CL, CM, CN
% CN-HK, CN-MO, CN-TW, CO, CR, CU, CV, CW, CX, CY, CZ, DE
% DJ, DK, DM, DO, DZ, EC, EE, EG, EH, ER, ES, ET
% FI, FJ, FK, FM, FO, FR, FR-971, FR-972, FR-973, FR-974, FR-976, FR-MF
% FR-NC, FR-PF, FR-PM, FR-TF, FR-WF, GA, GB, GD, GE, GG, GH, GI
% GL, GM, GN, GQ, GR, GS, GT, GU, GW, GY, HM, HN
% HR, HT, HU, ID, IE, IL, IM, IN, IO, IQ, IR, IS
% IT, JE, JM, JO, JP, KE, KG, KH, KI, KM, KN, KP
% KR, KW, KY, KZ, LA, LB, LC, LI, LK, LR, LS, LT
% LU, LV, LY, MA, MC, MD, ME, MG, MH, MK, ML, MM
% MN, MP, MR, MS, MT, MU, MV, MW, MX, MY, MZ, NA
% NE, NF, NG, NI, NL, NL-SX, NO, NO-21, NP, NR, NU, NZ
% OM, PA, PE, PG, PH, PK, PL, PN, PR, PS, PT, PW
% PY, QA, RO, RS, RU, RW, SA, SB, SC, SD, SE, SG
% SH, SI, SK, SL, SM, SN, SO, SR, SS, ST, SV, SY
% SZ, TC, TD, TG, TH, TJ, TK, TL, TM, TN, TO, TR
% TT, TV, TZ, UA, UG, UM, US, US-VI, UY, UZ, VA, VC
% VE, VG, VN, VU, WS, YE, ZA, ZM, ZW
